\title{\vspace{-1.2em}\Large\bfseries Single-Channel Fetal QRS Detection:\\ Where the Performance Actually Comes From}
\author{Ngo Binh Minh$^{1}$, [Co-author]$^{1}$, [Supervisor]$^{1}$\\[3pt]
\normalsize $^{1}$Faculty of Information Technology, Ton Duc Thang University, Ho Chi Minh City, Vietnam}
\date{}

\begin{document}
\twocolumn[
\begin{@twocolumnfalse}
\maketitle
\vspace{-2.0em}
\begin{center}
\begin{minipage}{0.92\textwidth}
\section*{\centering\normalsize Abstract}
\small\itshape
Deep detectors for fetal QRS complexes in single-channel abdominal ECG are usually reported as a
whole, so it is unclear which design choices produce the accuracy. We hold the evaluation protocol
fixed (leave-one-record-out, $\pm$50\ms\ tolerance, decision threshold chosen on an inner validation
record, label-blind lead selection) and vary one factor at a time. A dilated temporal convolutional
network with 113\,481 parameters, trained on five labour recordings of ADFECGDB, reaches a macro F1 of
99.21\% on the label-blind lead and 97.45\% averaged over four leads. Applied zero-shot to ten
pregnancy recordings of the Silesian database (32--42 weeks, same recorder) it scores
93.30\,$\pm$\,13.46\%; on ten records of the CinC 2013 set-a (different recorder) it falls to 59.15\%
(PSD lead) or 77.34\% (fixed lead). A three-tier decomposition attributes the gain over a naive
pipeline first to the signal front-end (10--60\Hz\ band: +11.00 F1), then to temporal context and
per-sample output (+4.53, $p<0.001$), and last to the architecture family at fixed context (+0.41,
$p=0.70$). Under the same protocol six architectures lie within 1.5 F1 points and none differs
significantly from a dilated CNN. Against re-implemented classical baselines the network adds
+11.06 points over peak picking on the same residual. A reject gate built from model confidence and
rhythm regularity (cross-dataset AUROC 0.929) raises F1 by 7.4 points at 80\% coverage;
persistent-homology features give AUROC 0.566, a negative result we report in full.
\end{minipage}
\end{center}
\vspace{1.2em}
\end{@twocolumnfalse}
]

\section{Introduction}
Fetal heart rate from abdominal electrodes needs a reliable fetal QRS (fQRS) detector, and a
single-channel detector keeps the hardware small. The PhysioNet/CinC Challenge 2013 fixed the
metric still in use, F1 with a $\pm$50\ms\ window \cite{clifford2014}, and Behar et al.\
benchmarked template subtraction, Kalman and blind-source methods under it, grid-searching the
band-pass filter as part of the method \cite{behar2014}. Andreotti et al.\ showed with a synthetic
stress-test that pre-processing choices change the ranking of extraction algorithms
\cite{andreotti2016}. Since the first convolutional fQRS detector \cite{zhong2018}, deep single-channel
methods have been reported with many simultaneous changes: dual-path source separation trained only
on synthetic data (98.08\% F1 on the ADFECGDB labour group, zero-shot) \cite{shokouhmand2023}, a
classical matched-filter pipeline with power-spectral-density (PSD) lead selection (99.5\,$\pm$\,0.5\% on
Silesian pregnancy records, 98.0\,$\pm$\,3.0\% in labour, four channels) \cite{jaeger2024}, and
deep-learning-guided empirical mode decomposition with a 10--60\Hz\ band \cite{xu2026}.

Each of these papers changes the band, the sampling rate, the temporal context, the architecture and
sometimes the lead-selection rule at once, and the lead is often chosen with the test labels. None
holds the protocol fixed and asks which of these choices carries the performance. We do that. The
contributions are:
\begin{itemize}\setlength{\itemsep}{0pt}
\item a leakage-free single-channel protocol with a three-tier decomposition of the gain into
  front-end, temporal context and architecture family;
\item an eight-architecture benchmark under that protocol, showing that the family does not matter
  while the receptive field does;
\item cross-dataset evidence on 22 independent women (after a signal-level duplicate check) that
  generalisation breaks across recorders rather than across gestation, plus a reject gate with a
  controlled negative result for topological signal-quality features.
\end{itemize}

\section{Methods}
\subsection{Data}
\textbf{ADFECGDB} \cite{goldberger2000}: five labour recordings (r01, r04, r07, r08, r10), four
abdominal leads at 1\,kHz, 5\,min each, with fQRS annotations from a scalp electrode (3\,191 beats).
\textbf{Silesian database} \cite{matonia2020}: ten pregnancy recordings (B1, 32--42 weeks, 20\,min,
199.6\,min in total) and twelve labour recordings (B2, 5\,min), four abdominal leads at 500\,Hz, same
hospital and recorder as ADFECGDB. B1 annotations are indirect (no scalp electrode; author pipeline
plus expert correction), and in 5/10 records they sit systematically 8--12\ms\ after the abdominal R
peak; B2 annotations come from a scalp electrode. Before evaluation we cross-correlated every
ADFECGDB record against every B2 record at 250\,Hz over all $4\times4$ lead pairs and $\pm$5\,min lags:
five B2 records are the ADFECGDB records (B2\_01\,=\,r01, B2\_02\,=\,r10, B2\_07\,=\,r04,
B2\_10\,=\,r07, B2\_11\,=\,r08; normalised cross-correlation 0.988--0.994). The independent set is
therefore 22 women (5 ADFECGDB, 10 B1, 7 new B2); the five duplicates are scored only with the fold
model that never saw them. \textbf{CinC 2013 set-a} \cite{clifford2014}: records a01--a10, four
channels at 1\,kHz, 1\,min each, a different recorder.

\subsection{Detector}
One abdominal lead is band-passed 10--60\Hz\ (4th-order Butterworth, zero-phase) with a 50\Hz\ notch
and resampled to 250\,Hz. Maternal QRS are located on an 8--25\Hz\ energy envelope and removed by
subtracting a median beat template with per-beat least-squares scaling. The residual and the filtered
signal, each robust-scaled by median and interquartile range, form a two-channel input of
4\,s (1\,000 samples). The network is a dilated residual temporal convolutional network (TCN): a stem
and five residual blocks (dilations 1, 2, 4, 8, 16; 40 channels; kernel 7; BatchNorm; GELU) with a
$1\times1$ output convolution, 113\,481 trainable parameters, receptive field 379 samples
(1\,516\ms). It emits one logit per input sample; the target is a Gaussian heat-map
($\sigma=3$ samples) around each annotated fQRS. Training uses binary cross-entropy with positive
class weighting, AdamW (learning rate $3\times10^{-3}$, one-cycle schedule, weight decay $10^{-4}$),
six epochs, batch 32, segments cut with a 1\,s stride. At inference, windows overlap by 1\,s and
probabilities are averaged; peaks above a threshold with a 250\ms\ refractory period are the fQRS.
The checkpoint is 0.48\,MB and one 4\,s window takes 4.35\ms\ on a desktop CPU
(920$\times$ real time).

\subsection{Protocol}
Leave-one-record-out (LORO) on ADFECGDB with five folds: one test record, one inner validation record
(the first of the remaining four) and three training records. The decision threshold is chosen on the
validation record from a grid 0.20--0.75 and applied unchanged to the test record. Detections are
matched one-to-one to annotations with greedy nearest matching within $\pm$50\ms; macro F1 is the mean
over the 20 (record$\times$lead) evaluations. Because a clinical device sees one lead, we also report a
\emph{label-blind} lead: following Power-MF \cite{jaeger2024}, the lead whose residual envelope has
the largest Welch PSD peak in 1.8--3.0\Hz\ (108--180\,bpm). This rule never touches annotations.
Silesian and CinC records are scored zero-shot with the model trained on all five ADFECGDB records
(threshold = median of the fold thresholds). Paired comparisons use the Wilcoxon signed-rank test on
the 20 (record$\times$lead) scores.

\subsection{Experiments}
\textbf{Classical baselines}, re-implemented and scored with the same matcher: template subtraction
(TS) and TS with a two-component principal-component template (TS-PCA) \cite{behar2014}, each
followed by an adaptive Pan--Tompkins detector \cite{pan1985}; and \emph{Prominence}, our own
front-end followed by \texttt{scipy} peak picking (prominence above median\,$+\,3$\,MAD, 250\ms\
spacing). Hyper-parameters were tuned on r01 only and frozen for the other four records.

\textbf{Three-tier decomposition.} Tier 1 (front-end): eight literature band-pass settings, LORO,
gradient-boosted trees on 300\ms\ windows of two leads. Tier 2 (temporal context and output
parameterisation): a 300\ms\ window classifier versus the 4\,s per-sample TCN, four leads. Tier 3
(architecture family at fixed context): gradient boosting versus a dilated CNN on the same 300\ms\
windows. A receptive-field sweep of the TCN (172--3\,052\ms) isolates context within one family.

\textbf{Architecture benchmark.} Eight families (linear, MLP, plain CNN, dilated CNN, large CNN,
CNN-GRU, TCN, ResNet-1D) trained on 300\ms\ windows, band 10--60\Hz, LORO with threshold chosen on the
validation record, one seed, three epochs, $\pm$50\ms\ scoring.

\textbf{Reject gate.} Each 4\,s segment is labelled \emph{bad} if the detector's F1 on it is below 80.
A classifier is trained on 1\,500 ADFECGDB segments (fold models) and tested on 600 CinC 2013
segments (zero-shot model). Feature groups: 16 topological features (Takens embedding, dimension 3,
$H_0$/$H_1$ persistence statistics \cite{perea2015,adams2017}, and sublevel-set $H_0$ of the 1-D
residual), 12 classical features (6 signal-only: sample entropy, kurtosis, spectral entropy,
10--60\Hz\ energy ratio, fetal-band PSD peak, autocorrelation lag; 6 model-output: RR coefficient of
variation, plausible-RR fraction, bSQI, detection count, mean peak probability, maximum probability),
and their union. The learner (logistic regression or gradient boosting) is picked by inner
cross-validation on ADFECGDB only. Rejecting the least-confident segments gives a risk--coverage
curve; bootstrap over 1\,000 resamples.

\section{Results}
\subsection{Detection across five data configurations}
Table~\ref{tab:data} gives the same model under the same $\pm$50\ms\ protocol on five configurations.
On ADFECGDB the label-blind PSD rule reaches 99.21\%, identical to the label-using oracle lead in every
record, and 99.18\% (Se 99.59, PPV 98.79, mean timing error 3.05\ms) on the leads prescribed by the
DPSS benchmark \cite{shokouhmand2023}. Across four leads the LORO macro F1 is 97.43\,$\pm$\,4.37\% (Se
97.69, PPV 97.18, jitter 3.62\ms; 12\,468 TP, 366 FP, 296 FN) and 97.16\,$\pm$\,4.34\% over three seeds.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering\footnotesize
\caption{Macro F1 (\%) of one model, $\pm$50\ms, on five data configurations. SD across records.
``$\ge$90''/``$<$50'': number of records with PSD-lead F1 in that range.}
\label{tab:data}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lccccc@{}}
\toprule
Configuration & $n$ & PSD lead & 4-lead mean & $\ge$90 & $<$50\\
\midrule
ADFECGDB, LORO & 5 & 99.21\,$\pm$\,1.50 & 97.45\,$\pm$\,2.96 & 5 & 0\\
Silesia B2 labour, all & 12 & 97.17\,$\pm$\,7.45 & 95.26\,$\pm$\,9.59 & 11 & 0\\
\quad 7 new women, zero-shot & 7 & 95.87\,$\pm$\,9.75 & 93.73\,$\pm$\,12.48 & 6 & 0\\
Silesia B1 pregnancy, zero-shot & 10 & 93.30\,$\pm$\,13.46 & 91.31\,$\pm$\,9.99 & 8 & 0\\
CinC 2013 set-a, zero-shot & 10 & 59.15\,$\pm$\,37.17 & 61.96\,$\pm$\,34.09 & 4 & 5\\
\quad fixed lead 0 & 10 & 77.34\,$\pm$\,28.54 & -- & 5 & 3\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Zero-shot on Silesian pregnancy records the model keeps 93.30\%; eight of ten records exceed 90 and
none falls below 50 (oracle lead 97.04\%). The two weakest B1 records are those where the PSD rule
picks the worst lead. On CinC 2013 the distribution is bimodal: with a fixed lead, five records are
above 90 and three below 50; the PSD rule, which equalled the oracle in-domain, picks the wrong lead
out-of-domain and loses 18 points (59.15 vs 77.34).

\subsection{Where the performance comes from}
Table~\ref{tab:tiers} decomposes the gain. Changing only the band from 1--45\Hz\ to 10--60\Hz\ adds
11.00 points; the spread over eight literature bands is 18.89 points (72.17 for 0.5--100\Hz\ to
91.06 for 10--60\Hz). Moving from a 300\ms\ window classifier to the 4\,s per-sample TCN adds 4.53
points ($p<0.001$); this step bundles receptive field, output parameterisation and parameter count
(33\,k to 113\,k). Replacing gradient boosting by a dilated CNN at the same 300\ms\ context adds 0.41
points ($p=0.70$). Within the TCN family, LORO F1 rises from 92.67\,$\pm$\,10.46 at a 172\ms\ receptive
field to 97.16\,$\pm$\,4.34 at 1\,516\ms\ (three seeds, $p<0.001$); on the validation record the sweep
reads 98.73, 99.25, 99.50 and 99.52 for 172, 748, 1\,516 and 3\,052\ms, so the benefit saturates near
1.5\,s.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering\footnotesize
\caption{Three-tier decomposition on ADFECGDB, LORO, $\pm$50\ms. Tier 1 uses two leads, tiers 2--3
four leads. Paired Wilcoxon on (record$\times$lead) scores; tier 1 was not tested pairwise.}
\label{tab:tiers}
\begin{tabular}{@{}llccc@{}}
\toprule
Tier & Change & From & To & $\Delta$F1 ($p$)\\
\midrule
1 Front-end & band 1--45 $\to$ 10--60\Hz\ (GBM, 300\ms) & 80.06 & 91.06 & +11.00\\
2 Context & 300\ms\ window CNN $\to$ 4\,s per-sample TCN & 92.90 & 97.43 & +4.53 ($<$0.001)\\
3 Family & GBM $\to$ dilated CNN, 300\ms\ fixed & 92.49 & 92.90 & +0.41 (0.70)\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Classical baselines}
Table~\ref{tab:base} shows the network gains 11.06 points over peak picking on exactly the residual it
receives, winning 20/20 paired evaluations ($p=1.9\times10^{-6}$); it also more than halves the
spread across women and leads (SD 4.44 vs 10.17 for the best baseline). TS-PCA on the PSD lead reaches
96.74, only 2.47 points below the network, consistent with the strength of purely classical
pipelines \cite{jaeger2024}.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering\footnotesize
\caption{Re-implemented baselines on ADFECGDB, $\pm$50\ms, hyper-parameters tuned on r01 only.
Columns: macro F1 over 20 (record$\times$lead), over the 16 evaluations excluding r01, and on the
PSD lead (5 records); difference to the TCN with Wilcoxon $p$ and number of losing pairs.}
\label{tab:base}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccc@{}}
\toprule
Method & 4 leads (20) & Held-out (16) & PSD lead (5) & $\Delta$ ($p$; lost)\\
\midrule
TS + Pan--Tompkins & 78.96\,$\pm$\,23.52 & 75.55 & 87.68 & $-18.49$ ($5.7{\times}10^{-6}$; 19/20)\\
TS-PCA + Pan--Tompkins & 91.05\,$\pm$\,10.17 & 90.94 & 96.74 & $-6.40$ ($1.9{\times}10^{-6}$; 20/20)\\
Prominence, same front-end & 86.39\,$\pm$\,11.14 & 84.99 & 92.03 & $-11.06$ ($1.9{\times}10^{-6}$; 20/20)\\
FetalQRS-TCN (ours) & \textbf{97.45}\,$\pm$\,4.44 & \textbf{96.81} & \textbf{99.21} & --\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Architecture family}
Figure~\ref{fig:arch} shows the eight-family benchmark. Six families (large CNN 92.43, dilated CNN
91.70, MLP 91.64, CNN-GRU 91.59, TCN 91.07, ResNet-1D 90.90) lie within 1.53 points and none differs
significantly from the dilated CNN (all $p\ge0.105$). Only the plain CNN with the same parameter count
but no dilation (88.76, $p=0.0001$) and the linear model (37.47) are different. All eight, limited to
300\ms\ of context, stay below the 97.43 of the 4\,s per-sample model.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figs/fig13_kien_truc_v2.pdf}
\caption{Eight architectures under the same protocol (10--60\Hz, 300\ms\ windows, LORO, threshold on
validation, one seed, three epochs). Bars: macro F1 over 20 (record$\times$lead) with SD; right-hand
columns: F1, Wilcoxon $p$ against the dilated CNN (\emph{m\^oc} = reference), trainable parameters.
Dashed line: the 4\,s per-sample TCN (97.43).}
\label{fig:arch}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Reject gate and a negative topological result}
Table~\ref{tab:gate} and Figure~\ref{fig:rc} give the cross-dataset reject gate. The 12 classical
features predict bad segments with AUROC 0.929 and lift the retained-segment F1 from 61.96 at full
coverage to 69.40 at 80\% coverage (random rejection: 62.03; oracle: 74.55) and to 84.51 at 50\%
coverage (oracle 97.81). The strongest single features are the model's own maximum probability
(AUROC 0.970), mean peak probability (0.956), RR coefficient of variation (0.915) and the 10--60\Hz\
energy ratio (0.904). The 16 topological features reach AUROC 0.566, 1.4 points above random rejection
at 80\% coverage, and adding them to the classical set lowers AUROC to 0.905. Even the six signal-only
classical features (0.666) beat topology.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering\footnotesize
\caption{Bad-segment prediction on CinC 2013 (600 segments) by a classifier trained on ADFECGDB
(1\,500 segments). F1: macro F1 of retained segments at the given coverage.}
\label{tab:gate}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}
\toprule
Feature set & AUROC & F1 @ 80\% cov. & F1 @ 50\% cov.\\
\midrule
Random rejection & 0.500 & 62.03 & --\\
Topological (16) & 0.566 & 63.40 & --\\
Classical, signal-only (6) & 0.666 & -- & --\\
Classical (12) & \textbf{0.929} & \textbf{69.40} & \textbf{84.51}\\
Combined (28) & 0.905 & -- & --\\
Oracle (true F1) & -- & 74.55 & 97.81\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figs/fig9_risk_coverage.png}
\caption{Risk--coverage curves on CinC 2013 for a gate trained on ADFECGDB. Dashed: oracle; grey:
random rejection; blue: topological features; orange: classical features; green: combined.}
\label{fig:rc}
\end{figure}

Three earlier controls point the same way. As a detector input, a persistence-image CNN scored 27.42
where a 1-D CNN with the same parameter budget scored 97.14, and a two-stream fusion added 0.88 points
($p=0.625$). The sublevel-set $H_0$ persistence of a 1-D signal equals classical peak prominence
(Elder rule); we verified zero difference against \texttt{scipy} on 2\,011 peaks, so those five
features are a re-parameterisation of prominence. And 8 of the 16 topological features change the sign
of their Spearman correlation with segment F1 between ADFECGDB and CinC 2013 (3 of 12 classical
features do). We therefore report the topological signal-quality index as a negative result.

\section{Discussion}
\textbf{The front-end dominates.} Of the 15.9 points separating the naive pipeline from the final
model, 11.0 come from the pass-band alone; the same band also appears as the strongest signal-only
error predictor. This matches the observation of \cite{andreotti2016} that pre-processing reorders
methods, and it means that architecture comparisons run under different bands are not comparisons of
architectures.

\textbf{The family does not matter; the receptive field does.} Six families are statistically
indistinguishable at fixed context, while dilation ($+2.95$ at equal parameters) and a longer
receptive field (172 to 1\,516\ms: $+4.5$ LORO) are the only architectural variables with a
measurable effect. Roughly one fetal RR interval and its neighbours is what the detector needs.

\textbf{Generalisation breaks across recorders, not gestation.} Trained on five labour records, the
model loses about four points on pregnancy records from the same recorder (97 to 93) and 20--38
points on a different recorder (97 to 77 or 59). The PSD lead rule equals the oracle in-domain but
collapses out-of-domain, so a learned gate should replace the spectral heuristic. The gate we tested
is such a replacement: it needs no labels at test time and recovers 7.4 points at 80\% coverage.

\textbf{Why topology failed here.} Sublevel-set persistence on a 1-D signal is prominence, which the
classical set already contains; delay-embedding $H_1$ features depend on residual maternal amplitude
and muscle noise, which differ between recorders, and their correlation sign flips; and the dominant
failure mode is rhythmic (locking onto maternal beats over seconds) rather than morphological
(milliseconds), so rhythm regularity and model confidence are the informative quantities. Computing
cost is not the obstacle (65\ms\ per segment).

\textbf{Limitations.} Training uses five women, all in labour at 38--41 weeks, and one seed for the
main model (three seeds for the receptive-field claim). B1 labels are indirect, so B1 F1 measures
agreement with the authors' pipeline, and its timing error (8.2\ms) is not comparable with B2. CinC
2013 results rest on ten 1-min records and are bimodal, so record-level confidence intervals are
wide. The architecture benchmark uses one seed and three epochs. Published numbers of DPSS and
Power-MF are quoted, not re-run: Power-MF needs four channels and MATLAB dependencies not in its
repository. Only one topological configuration was tried, deliberately without tuning on test labels.

\section{Conclusion}
With protocol held fixed, single-channel fQRS detection on ADFECGDB reaches 99.21\% F1 on a
label-blind lead and 93.30\% zero-shot on pregnancy recordings, and the gain over a naive pipeline
comes from the 10--60\Hz\ front-end and from 1.5\,s of temporal context, not from the network family.
Cross-recorder transfer, not gestational age, is the open problem; a confidence-and-rhythm reject
gate mitigates it, and persistent-homology features do not. Code, checkpoints and logs are available
from the authors.

